# WHY this file is COMMITTED (2026-08-09): it existed only as an untracked local
# file, so its protection applied to exactly one machine. On any other clone — or
# after a fresh checkout — a `git add -A` would have staged operator scratch and
# local scan state into this PUBLIC repository. Committing it makes the guard real
# for everyone. (Same failure class as the 2026-07-08 monorepo near-miss.)

# ADR-0044: scratch space for commit messages + PR bodies authored as files.
# Files in these directories are ignored; the .gitkeep files document the pattern.
/.commit-messages/*
!/.commit-messages/.gitkeep
/.pr-bodies/*
!/.pr-bodies/.gitkeep

# WHY: PullGuard's own local scan state, written when someone scans this repo from
# their workstation. It is machine-local scratch here — this repo runs no scan
# workflow, so there is no cross-run first-seen history to preserve — and a
# committed scorecard of our own public repo is not something to publish. Mirrors
# the monorepo's .gitignore treatment of the same files. (A repo that DOES scan
# itself in CI commits these deliberately so first-seen tracking survives; that is
# a per-repo decision, and the answer here is no.)
.drift-cache.json
.drift-trend.json
.drift-signatures.json
.drift-baseline.json
.drift-history.json
.drift-scan-details.json
